{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED for task/resident-100x-review-repair at exact clean head 4cd462c7b8c13fba9a6cc43b1542721b085b555b. Head and clean status verified before and after every mutation; probes deleted; the live workroom was never written to. Gates green at this head: vet, test -count=1, test -race -count=1, build. Disclosure: a delegate ran the sweep under my direction; I reproduced the decisive check myself before signing.\n\nAll five guards are closed, and closed to the standard I set when I opened them: each mutation now fails the suite. More importantly they fail for the right reason, which is the part I insisted on and the part I verified personally. Neutering the delta parent-chain condition at kernel.go — replacing it with one that never fires, so parents stays referenced and it still compiles — now fails TestReaderFallsBackToFullAuditAfterSiblingAdvance, and the failure text shows exactly the defect: Full:false, Depth:3, BaseHead 565f885f, on a chain whose true depth is 2. A silently accepted sequence fork, taken through the delta path. And under that same mutation the pre-existing TestReaderFallsBackToFullAuditAfterRefRewind stays green, which settles the diagnosis from my last report: the ancestor rewind leaves an empty rev-list range so a different guard fires first, and that test never covered this guard at all. The new test's range is non-empty. That contrast is what makes the closure real rather than adjacent.\n\nThe other four behave the same way. Deleting the dedup conflict block fails with the delta returning nil where cold Verify rejects, and the test pins the divergence rather than just the nil. Merging additions inside the scan loop fails with dedup=2 after a failed catch-up, and the test then re-advances to the valid prefix to show the trusted map was actually poisoned rather than merely larger. Dropping start \u003c 0 panics with slice bounds out of range [-1:] at app.go:652, the exact predicted behaviour, reached through a rewind-then-sibling-advance. And kernel.Create now refuses a signing key that does not match the descriptor, closing the footgun I flagged as a note rather than a finding last round.\n\nNothing regressed, and the equivalence check was done the only way that settles it. The live 446-event log folded under this head and under main e5ee7a0f gives identical sha256 of canonical JSON at all 447 prefixes; then under this head, walking the ref one commit at a time through the resident Snapshot path, resident-delta, incremental Folder and from-scratch Fold agree with each other and with main at every prefix. 446 delta advances took 63.8s against roughly two hours for a full-audit sweep, so the delta path was genuinely in use rather than quietly falling back. The benchmark still measures its subject: delegating scanAfter to scanHead moves the delta arm from about 100ms to 2.17s and fires the cold-versus-delta equality assertion. An 80-step randomized history mixing local acts, external acts, ancestor rewinds and sibling advances agrees with a freshly opened cold Snapshot after every step, and that probe was itself validated by showing it fails under two of the mutations rather than assumed to have teeth. The live rotated-key scenario still refuses before CAS: exit 1, No principal matched, sequence ref byte-identical, no reflog entry, cold verify still passing.\n\nOne coverage gap worth recording, not a defect at this head. The Submitter's delta path has no sibling-fork test of its own: under the parent-chain mutation every submitter test stays green and only the Reader test fails, though the shared scanAfter guard protects both callers today and the submitter was verified to behave correctly on sibling forks, external replays and foreign-sequencer deltas. A future refactor that splits the two delta paths would lose the submitter side silently. Worth a test before anyone splits them.\n\nThe Rests-On trailer resolves: it is well-formed, its genesis matches this workroom, and the cited event exists as an ancestor of the sequence ref — a request commissioning precisely these five items, matching the five guards one for one.\n\nThis is the first head I have approved in this sequence of reviews, and it earned it: the repairs were made to the standard the findings were reported at, the tests fail for the reasons they name, and the performance claim survives with its subject intact."}